The '''2026 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2025–26 UEFA Champions League]], the 71st season of Europe's premier club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 34th season since it was renamed from the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Puskás Aréna]] in [[Budapest]], Hungary,<ref name="appointment"/> on 30 May 2026 between French club [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]], the defending champions, and English club [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]. This was the first final to include a titleholder team since [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]], as well as Arsenal’s first [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|Champions League Final]] appearance since [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]], where they lost to [[FC Barcelona]] 2–1.

Paris Saint-Germain won the match 4–3 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]], following a 1–1 draw after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], for their second UEFA Champions League title, becoming the second club to win back-to-back titles in the UEFA Champions League era, matching [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s feat in the [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League|2016–17 edition]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/02a5-20c02e59f219-15e92acdc717-1000/ |title=Paris retain Champions League: Holders edge Arsenal on penalties |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=30 May 2026 |access-date=30 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Champions League holders: Paris join Real Madrid as back-to-back winners |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e96d80bc2f6-d4e7f311245e-1000/ |date=30 May 2026 |access-date=31 May 2026 |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> As winners, Paris Saint-Germain earned the right to play against [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]], the winners of the [[2025–26 UEFA Europa League]], in the [[2026 UEFA Super Cup]], winning the title. They will also compete in the final of the [[2026 FIFA Intercontinental Cup]], and qualified for the [[2029 FIFA Club World Cup]].

==Background==
[[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] reached their third European Cup/Champions League final overall and second consecutively, having lost to [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020]] before winning the title in [[2025 UEFA Champions League final|2025]]. They also became the first team since [[Liverpool FC|Liverpool]] in [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]] and [[2019 UEFA Champions League final|2019]] to reach two consecutive Champions League finals. This was their seventh UEFA competition final overall, having also triumphed in the [[1996 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final]] and [[2025 UEFA Super Cup]]. They lost the Cup Winners' Cup final in [[1997 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1997]] as holders, as well as losing the [[1996 UEFA Super Cup]]. Paris Saint-Germain was the first French team to appear in three European Cup/Champions League finals, surpassing their [[Le Classique|rivals]] [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]], who lost in [[1991 European Cup final|1991]] before their victory in [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]], as well as [[Stade de Reims|Stade Reims]], who lost both the [[1956 European Cup final|1956]] and [[1959 European Cup final|1959]] European Cup finals to [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]].

[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] reached their second European Cup/Champions League final, having lost to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]]. This was their eighth UEFA competition final overall, having triumphed in the [[1994 European Cup Winners' Cup final]]. They lost the Cup Winners' Cup finals in [[1980 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1980]] and [[1995 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1995]]—the latter as holders, as well as losing the [[1994 European Super Cup]], [[2000 UEFA Cup final]], and [[2019 UEFA Europa League final]].

Both clubs won their respective leagues and were eliminated from other cup competitions, thus aiming for a [[Double (association football)#Continental double|continental double]]. This was the first time since [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020]] where both Champions League finalists were champions of their respective domestic leagues. The two teams met in the 2025 semi-finals, with Paris Saint-Germain winning both legs (1–0 at the [[Emirates Stadium]] and then 2–1 at the [[Parc des Princes]]).

The 2026 final was the fourth European Cup final between sides from two different capital cities after [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]] and [[1971 European Cup final|1971]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Anand |first=Anish |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48699334/uefa-champions-league-stats-kvaratskhelia-record-seven-game-streak-dembele-16-goal-contributions-arsenal-unbeaten-14 |title=UCL stats: Kvaratskhelia's record seven-game streak, Dembélé's 16 goal contributions, Arsenal unbeaten in 14 |work=ESPN |date=7 May 2026 }}</ref>

The "home" team (for administrative purposes) was predetermined as the winners of semi-final 1 (Paris Saint-Germain). This was the first Champions League final to kick off at 18:00 [[Central European Summer Time|CEST]], three hours earlier than previous editions. UEFA stated that the change was made to "enhance the matchday experience and benefit fans, teams and host cities".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/029c-1e9620d873ad-b9d62ed347c6-1000/ |title=UEFA Champions League final kick-off moves to 18:00CET as of 2025/26 season |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=28 August 2025 |access-date=17 September 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Zelealem |first1=Fasika |last2=Davies |first2=Mathew |date=6 May 2026 |title=When is the Champions League final? Location and ticket information |url=https://www.walesonline.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/champions-league-final-location-ticket-33896709 |access-date=30 May 2026 |website=Wales Online |language=en}}</ref>

