==Aftermath==
{{Main|2026 Paris Saint-Germain celebration riots}}
Following PSG's victory, tens of thousands of fans celebrated on the streets of Paris. Violent clashes erupted as the celebrations developed into rioting; there were 283 arrests in Paris and another 133 in the rest of France.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 31, 2026 |title=Hundreds arrested in Paris after Champions League final |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cg7pk5j7nyjo |access-date=2026-04-06 |website=BBC |language=en-GB|last=Sekulich|first=Harry}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/france/article/2026/05/30/dozens-arrested-in-paris-clashes-on-psg-victory-night_6753979_7.html|title=Dozens arrested in Paris clashes on PSG victory night |website=[[Le Monde]] |date=May 30, 2026|access-date=4 June 2026}}</ref> The [[Minister of the Interior (France)|French Interior Ministry]] deployed 22,000 security officers across the country, including 8,000 in the capital. Similar riots [[2025 Paris Saint-Germain celebration riots|had occurred the previous year]] following [[2025 UEFA Champions League final#Celebrations and riots|PSG's victory in the 2025 final]].<ref>{{cite web | title=Jubilant PSG supporters spill onto Paris streets, some clashes with police | work=[[Reuters]] | url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/jubilant-psg-supporters-spill-onto-paris-streets-some-clashes-with-police-2026-05-30/ |date=30 May 2026|access-date=4 June 2026}}</ref>

